From d0cd9c8d0fd59bc7d140f3d60cf02e1d80376dab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Praveen Talari <praveen.talari@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 2 Jul 2026 17:11:50 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume to system sleep callbacks

commit d0cd9c8d0fd59bc7d140f3d60cf02e1d80376dab upstream (v7.3-rc1), with
its fix 3098c989bd38edff871d798acd7f1d1b269f7edd upstream (v7.3-rc6,
"serial: qcom-geni: Fix unbalanced runtime PM resume for no_console_suspend",
Abel Vesa) folded in.

During system sleep the hardware resources (clocks, interconnect) are
not gated because the runtime-suspend callback is never invoked from
the system sleep path.  This prevents the platform from reaching its
lowest idle state.

The system sleep callbacks qcom_geni_serial_suspend() and
qcom_geni_serial_resume() rely solely on uart_suspend_port() /
uart_resume_port() to manage power.  uart_suspend_port() drives the
UART PM state machine to UART_PM_STATE_OFF, which in turn calls
pm_runtime_put_sync() and eventually the runtime-suspend callback.
However, if the runtime-PM usage count is still elevated at the time
of system sleep (e.g. the port is held active by an open file
descriptor), the runtime-suspend callback is never invoked and the
hardware resources (clocks, interconnect) remain enabled across
suspend, preventing the platform from reaching its lowest idle state.

Fix this by calling pm_runtime_force_suspend() at the end of
qcom_geni_serial_suspend() so that the runtime-suspend callback is
always executed regardless of the usage count, and by calling
pm_runtime_force_resume() at the start of qcom_geni_serial_resume()
to restore those resources before uart_resume_port() re-opens the
port.

Signed-off-by: Praveen Talari <praveen.talari@oss.qualcomm.com>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260702-add_force_suspend_resume_to_system_sleep_callbacks-v2-1-b79e254a7015@oss.qualcomm.com
Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman <gregkh@linuxfoundation.org>

The folded fix (3098c989bd38):

    When no_console_suspend is set for the serial console, the system
    suspend callback skips pm_runtime_force_suspend() to keep the console
    active. The resume callback still unconditionally calls
    pm_runtime_force_resume(), which triggers an "Unbalanced
    pm_runtime_enable!" warning.

    Only force resume when the suspend callback force suspended the device.

    Fixes: d0cd9c8d0fd5 ("serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume to system sleep callbacks")
    Signed-off-by: Abel Vesa <abel.vesa@oss.qualcomm.com>
    Reviewed-by: Praveen Talari <praveen.talari@oss.qualcomm.com>
    Tested-by: David Heidelberg <david@ixit.cz> # OnePlus 6T (sdm845)
    Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260828-serial-qcom-geni-fix-unbalanced-rpm-resume-v1-1-af3e5aef17a6@oss.qualcomm.com
    Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman <gregkh@linuxfoundation.org>

Port notes (this port's, not upstream's).  Both hunks are upstream's code
as it is in v7.3 and v7.4-rc4; only the line numbers are v7.2-rc3's.

Why: in kit revision 12's first sleep (SM-X710, 2026-11-24) the clock trace
found XO still voted at the entry to the sleep, held by two clocks alone:
gcc_qupv3_wrap1_s7_clk, uart7's (serial@a9c000, the console ttyMSM0), and
gcc_qupv3_wrap2_s6_clk, uart14's (serial@898000, the Bluetooth UART that
hci_qca opens through serdev).  In v7.2-rc3 both ports hold one runtime PM
reference while their serial core power state is UART_PM_STATE_ON: the
console's from uart_configure_port(), which leaves a console port on, the
Bluetooth UART's from uart_port_startup() at serdev's open.  In system
suspend uart_suspend_port() turns the state OFF, and qcom_geni_serial_pm()
drops that reference with pm_runtime_put_sync(); but device_prepare() holds
a reference of its own through the whole transition
(drivers/base/power/main.c, pm_runtime_get_noresume()), so the count does
not reach 0, the runtime suspend callback never runs, and
geni_se_resources_off() never unprepares the "se" clock.  Each port also
kept its QUP core bandwidth vote and its CX performance vote.

This tree keeps the uart_ops .pm callback (upstream removed it later, in
aee1f94dab13, v7.3-rc1, and fixed what that broke in 2de1066dc859 and
e95442c31ae6; none of the three is taken): uart_resume_port()'s
uart_change_pm(UART_PM_STATE_ON) is what powers the console port up again
when pm_runtime_force_suspend() found it not in use and left it suspended,
which is what e95442c31ae6 has to add without the callback.

Ordering with qcom-geni-serial-serve-irq-while-suspending.patch (SM-X710
builds; the interrupt handler, another function): uart_suspend_port() calls
shutdown(), whose disable_irq() waits for a running handler, before
uart_change_pm() and the force suspend turn the resources off; at the
resume they are on again before startup() enables the interrupt.  hci_qca
queues and does not send while it is suspending (QCA_SUSPENDING, from its
own suspend, which runs before the UART's, until its resume, after it), and
every console is marked suspended before the devices' suspend and until
after their resume (console_suspend_all(), console_resume_all()), so
nothing writes to an unclocked engine (console_suspend Y on both tablets).

Wakeup: uart_suspend_port() returns early, the port left running, for a tty
whose power/wakeup is enabled, and upstream's force suspend still powers it
down.  No wake source of this port uses that: ttyMSM0's power/wakeup reads
disabled (squirtle, 2026-11-24), neither platform device is wakeup capable
(no dedicated wake interrupt in the device tree), and the Bluetooth UART has
no tty device (serdev) and hci_qca installs no wakeup hook for it.  The
kit's verify.sh warns if ttyMSM0's wakeup is ever enabled.

Applied by scripts/build-mainline-kernel.sh with
APPLY_GENI_SERIAL_FORCE_SUSPEND=1, strictly, and refused for a build
without it in a tree that carries it.  It changes every system sleep on
both tablets, the boot's panel cycle (pm_test) included: test builds until
it has run on hardware.  No board gate: both tablets have the same two
UARTs (uart7 the console, uart14 the Bluetooth UART: WCN6855 on the
SM-X710, WCN7850 on the SM-X910).
---
 drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -1974,6 +1974,7 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(stru
 	struct qcom_geni_serial_port *port = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
+	int ret;
 
 	/*
 	 * This is done so we can hit the lowest possible state in suspend
@@ -1983,7 +1984,19 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(stru
 		geni_icc_set_tag(&port->se, QCOM_ICC_TAG_ACTIVE_ONLY);
 		geni_icc_set_bw(&port->se);
 	}
-	return uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+
+	ret = uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * When no_console_suspend is set the console must remain active
+	 * across system sleep, so skip the force suspend path.
+	 */
+	if (!console_suspend_enabled && uart_console(uport))
+		return 0;
+
+	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
 }
 
 static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
@@ -1993,6 +2006,12 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struc
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
 
+	if (console_suspend_enabled || !uart_console(uport)) {
+		ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
 	ret = uart_resume_port(private_data->drv, uport);
 	if (uart_console(uport)) {
 		geni_icc_set_tag(&port->se, QCOM_ICC_TAG_ALWAYS);
